    "from pytrends.request import TrendReq\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import os\n",
    "import time\n",
    "from google.colab import files"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "### Process:\n",
    "1. Process players in batches of 4 (Take 4 players from the list. Add the anchor to make it 5 total.)\n",
    "2. master_anchor_max → Lebron’s max for the first batch (reference for entire dataset)\n",
    "3. batch_anchor → Lebron’s max for each batch\n",
    "4. scale_factor = master_anchor_max / batch_anchor_max\n",
    "\n",
    "*   There will always be a peak at 100 for every batch(Google trends does not have the exact count, only has numbers relative to the highest point of the batch)\n",
    "*   If batch_anchor_max is lower than 100, it means that LeBron isn’t the highest, meaning someone else in the batch had the peak.\n",
    "*   So scale_factor > 1 means that someone else in the batch had a peak\n",
    "\n",
    "5. df_scaled = df * scale_factor →Multiply the entire batch by this scale factor\n",
    "*   Values can go over 100\n",
    "6. Append scaled df\n",
    "7. Find the actual global peak across all scaled data, then divide everything by that global peak, multiply by 100.\n",
    "*  all players are on the same scale\n"
   ],
   "metadata": {
    "id": "q4tqp6tYrx8v"
   }
  },
  {
